			      OS/8 <-> HOST HANDLER

     The HOST: handler allows PDP8/12 programmers using the OS/8-OS/12-OS/78
operating system to send files to, or receive files from, other DEC computer
systems running VMS, RSX or TOPS10/20 software.  All communication is through
the host system's terminal driver, hence no special transfer programs are re-
quired on the 'host' machine.  In fact, the files sent may include operating
system commands, making it possible to initiate processing on the host which
then runs in parallel with computations on the PDP8/12.

     Since the handler may be used with any OS/8 program, the resulting 'net-
work' is much more versatile than one implemented via special 'file-transfer'
programs on both ends.  The only restriction is that FORTRAN IV programs can
not use the handler for INPUT at the same time they are using any other non-
system handler for I/O operations.  This restriction stems from a design de-
ficiency in FRTS, which affects all handlers that retain information between
calls.  There are no restrictions on OUTPUT.

     The handler may be installed either by using the BUILD program (see the
OS/8 Handbook, Ch. 2) or by using the SET HANDLER command.  After the handler
is installed, the user may run the HOSTX program to change some of the para-
meters, for instance the device codes used, the 'concatenation switch' or the
'delay-time' setting; the use of the HOSTX program is described separately.
It is also necessary to have a 'terminal emulator' program available that al-
lows you to interact directly with the remote machine.  The TERMIN program
supplied with the handler is also described separately; it may be invoked by
the OS/78 '.TER' command.

     The HOST: handler has been designed to interface with the standard host
terminal driver.  However, it is best to set the following VMS terminal char-
acteristics before attempting to use the handler: SET TERM/TAB/FORM/WIDTH=132
/LOWER/TTSYNC/HOSTSYNC.  Of these, the last two are the most important since
they control the synchronization between the two machines.  'HOSTSYNC' means
that the host will respond to XON/XOFF signals sent from the PDP8/12, while
'TTSYNC' means that the host will send similar signals back to the '8.  This
command (or something equivalent) can be included in your LOGIN.COM file, or
it can be placed in the system startup file.  (Note that the 'TTSYNC' option
has only recently been implemented under RSX; the handler will thus NOT WORK
with earlier versions of that operating system.)


			    Sending files to the host

     To send files to the host one must first go through the usual LOGIN pro-
cedure and then issue a command to copy data into a host file:

		.R TERMIN		(or use .TER with OS/78)
		Username: ...
		Password: ...
		$ CREATE 'filespec'	(hit 'return', then Ctrl/E)

     On other systems 'CREATE' may be replaced by a 'COPY filespec=TT:' com-
mand (the ordering of input/output may differ).  This command should be ter-
minated by hitting the RETURN key, at which point any additional data sent
will be put in the file.  The next step is thus to exit from the TERMIN pro-
gram by typing 'Ctrl/E' to return to the OS/8 monitor:
		(Type Ctrl/E to exit from the TERMIN program)
		.COPY HOST:<filename	(this uses the FOTP program)
		FILES COPIED:
		filename		(only one at a time for now)
		.R TERMIN
		$ DIR filespec		(check that the file exists)
		$ TYPE filespec		(look at some of it)
		$ PRINT/delete filespec	(print it out and delete it)
		$ LOGOUT		(or whatever...)
		(Type Ctrl/E to return to OS/8)

     Aside from the login procedure, which is best performed interactively so
you can respond to mail, check messages, etc., the commands that create the
file can just as well be sent via the handler itself.  Thus after logging in,
you can immediately return to OS/8 to run the program which sends the CREATE
command (plus a Carriage Return), followed by the actual data.  This allows
'unattended' operation, (you can even run the program as a BATCH job!), which
is extremely convenient when moving many data files.

     Note that unlike almost all other 'non-file-structured' handlers, the
HOST: handler normally sends the 'CTRL/Z' which OS/8 uses to mark the end-of-
file.  Since the host also recognizes CTRL/Z as the EOF mark, this means the
receiving file will be closed automatically without any further attention.
If the file you are sending to the host is being generated by a program, the
operation of closing the OS/8 file will also 'close' the host file.  One can
also just send a CTRL/Z (octal 32, decimal 26) to close the host file without
closing the OS/8 file.  This allows the program to continue to use the HOST:
handler without the overhead of constantly re-loading it.

     This mode of operation is generally, but not always, convenient.  There
are times when it is desirable to merge several OS/8 files into a single host
file.  For example, one might like to create a temporary 'print file' to re-
ceive the output from a .COPY HOST:<*.LS command.  This won't work unless the
'no-EOF' switch in the handler is set, as otherwise the CTRL/Z at the end of
the first listing will close the host file.  The state of this switch may be
changed by altering location '100' in the handler, either with a 'SET HOST:
LOC 100=1' command, or more easily by using the HOSTX program:

		.R HOSTX/C		(Set the 'concatenate' switch)
		.R HOSTX/N		(Reset the switch to 'normal')

     A common use of the 'concatenated file' mode is to create a single host
file containing the OS/8 directory listings of many different floppy disks
or DECtapes.  These may then be conveniently printed all at once rather than
one-at-a-time.  To do this, run the TERMIN program and issue a $ CR DIR.LIS
command.  Then go back to OS/8 and proceed as follows:

		.R HOSTX/C			(combine output)
		.DIR HOST:<RXA1:/E/P		(first floppy)
		.DIR HOST:<RXA1:/E/P		(second disk)
		.DIR HOST:<DTA0:/E/P		(first tape, etc.)
		.R HOSTX/N			(reset EOF switch)
		.DIR HOST:<DTA1:/E/P		(do the last tape)
		.R TERMIN			(connect with host)
		$ PRINT/DELETE DIR		(print directory file)
		(type Ctrl/E to return to OS/8)
     Once one has learned the few simple steps required to use this handler,
the procedures shown above will become almost second nature.  The most fre-
quent mistake is forgetting to create the file on the host.  If this happens,
the data sent by the handler looks like a series of host commands (none of
which, hopefully, resembles a DELETE command!).  This situation can usually
be detected by noting when the transfer seems much slower than normal, due to
all the error messages being generated.


			Receiving files from the host

     The procedure for sending files from the host machine back to the PDP-8
is largely similar to that outlined for sending files the other way, with the
obvious switching of input/output operations.  One annoying difference on the
OS/8 side is that the .COPY command cannot be used - instead one must use the
.PUNCH command, due a deficiency in the FOTP program.  (PUNCH uses PIP rather
than FOTP.)  The basic outline of the steps required is as follows:

		1) Run the TERMIN program to login, etc.
		2) Issue a '$ TYPE filespec' command
		3) Type Ctrl/E to return to OS/8
		4) Call the HOST: handler as an input device

     Note especially that the TYPE command shown in Step 2 is but ONE method
for producing output from the host.  The procedure discussed below works with
ANY command which sends data to the terminal - thus the HOST: handler can be
used to 'capture' any sort of output, such as directory listings, mail, help
files, status reports, etc., in an OS/8 file.

     Since steps 3 & 4 typically take several seconds, it is obvious that un-
less something special is done in Step 2, most of the data sent to the PDP-8
will be lost before the HOST: handler can be called.  There are two ways to
temporarily stop execution of the host command: the first is to type the com-
mand as shown, but WITHOUT hitting the 'return' key.  This simply leaves the
command in the input buffer, waiting for the user to say 'go ahead and do it'.
This is the recommended procedure.  The second method (useful in case habit
overcomes reason and you hit the RETURN key) is to QUICKLY type Ctrl/S sever-
al times, which should stop output to the terminal.  This method is not com-
pletely reliable: sometimes the host appears to ignore this signal.

     With the host in a suspended state there is now time to invoke the OS/8
utility which will use the HOST: handler for input.  When the handler finally
receives an input request, it first sends a 'CTRL/Q' and then a 'RETURN', so
no matter how output was suspended, it will be sure to start.  Data received
from the host are then packed into the buffer area specified in the input re-
quest (using the standard OS/8 packing scheme) until either the buffer fills
up or the transmission ends.  If the buffer fills up first, the handler sends
XOFF to stop transmission until additional buffer space is available (buffer
space is determined by the calling program, not by the handler).  Eventually,
when the host stops sending data, the handler takes the 'EOF' exit.

     This raises an interesting question: how does the handler know when the
transmission is over?  The answer is: 'whenever no characters are received 
for a pre-determined period of time'.  Since the host does not send any 'EOF'
code to the terminal, this is the only way the handler can detect this con-
dition.  The 'pre-determined period of time' is an adjustable parameter in
the handler which must be set somewhat empirically, depending upon the re-
sponse time of a particular system.  The minimum delay is approximately 0.1
second, while delays of several seconds have been found necessary in cases
where the system is heavily loaded.  The 'delay time' may be set by changing
the value in location '300' of the handler, using either the 'SET LOC' com-
mand, or more conveniently, by using the '/T' option in the HOSTX program:

		.R HOSTX/T=3		(set the delay to '3')
		.R HOSTX/T=5		(increase it slightly)
		.R HOSTX/T=0		(reset to the default value)

It is desirable in general to keep this value as small as possible so that
the handler will respond quickly to a 'real' EOF condition, while not 'tim-
ing out' too soon when the system is busy.

     Note that the HOST: handler may be used 'simultaneously' for both input
and output.  Thus the calling program may use this handler to send commands
to the host and then wait for the calculated results to come back before pro-
ceeding.  To check out the 'receive' side of the handler, try sending a file
to the host and then copying it back to the PDP8/12:

		.R TERMIN		(connect terminal to host)
		$ CREATE TESTFILE	(set up temporary output file)
		(Type CTRL/E to return to OS/8)
		.COPY HOST:<TEST.DA

		.R TERMIN		(get back on the host)
		$ TYPE TESTFILE.	(Remember: Don't type RETURN!)
		(Type CTRL/E to get back to OS/8)
		.PUNCH TEST.TM<HOST:	(Remember: Can't use .COPY!)
		.COMPAR TEST.DA,TEST.TM	(Check for any differences)

     As you will discover by actually trying this example, the two files are
ALMOST, but not EXACTLY, identical.  The difference is that the file received
from the host contains an extra blank line plus a 'prompt' at the end.  These
spurious characters are sent immediately after the data, and hence get appen-
ded to the file.  To avoid this you can write a little command procedure that
puts a 2-second wait after the 'TYPE' command, allowing the handler to 'time-
out' before the command prompt:

		$ TYPE 'P1'		(list specified file on terminal)
		$ WAIT 0::2		(then wait for 2 seconds)

     If these two commands are put in a file called 'SEND8.COM', and you add
the definition $ SEND8:==@SEND8 to your login file, then you can use the com-
mand: '$ SEND8 filespec' in place of '$ TYPE filespec' to get an unblemished
version of the file.  Alternatively you can create a command procedure which
sends the file followed by a CTRL/Z.  (This is the better way to do things.)

		! PROCEDURE 'SENDZ'
		$ CTRLZ[0,32]=%X1A
		$ TYPE 'P1'
		$ WRITE SYS$OUTPUT CTRLZ

     Again you should add the definition SENDZ:==@SENDZ to your LOGIN file.
     Here's another example: suppose you would like to keep a running record
of the host time used to transfer files back and forth.  This information is
displayed by the LOGOUT command, so proceed as follows:

		.R TERMIN		(time to end this session)
		$ LOGOUT/FULL		(But don't hit the RETURN key!)
		(Type CTRL/E to go back to OS/8)
		.PU LOGOUT<LOGOUT,HOST:

     The .PUNCH command adds the new information to the end of the cumulative
LOGOUT file (which of course can be sent back to the host at some later time
for printing).

     As mentioned earlier, the HOST: handler can be used with essentially any
OS/8 program, not just with file-transfer programs like PIP and FOTP.  Here's
an example in which a host file is directly compared with an OS/8 file by the
SRCCOM program:

		.R TERMIN
		$ SENDZ filespec	(no RETURN, please)
		(Type CTRL/E to exit)
		.COMPARE original,HOST:	(do a direct comparison)
		first line of original
		first line of host file
		NO DIFFERENCES		(we hope!)


	    Using the HOST: handler with non-DEC operating systems

     In general any system which fully implements the 'DC1-DC3' (XON-XOFF)
protocol will work with this handler.  If the data transfer does NOT use the
normal terminal port, however, it may be necessary to fix the handler so it
will send Carriage Return/Linefeed (CR/LF) at the end of a line, not just CR.
This modification may be made by the HOSTX program:

		.R HOSTX/L		Allow LF after a CR
		.R HOSTX/N		No LF after a CR (normal)

     The VMS operating system effectively 'double-spaces' lines ending with
CRLF, hence the /N switch suppresses the LF after a CR (but not other LF's),
for 'normal' operation.
				Technical Details

     The key to successful communication between the PDP-8/12 and other oper-
ating systems lies in the use of the 'XON/XOFF' protocol.  According to this
scheme, the computer which is sending data must stop whenever it receives a
'XOFF' (DC3 or CTRL/S) character, resuming when it receives a 'XON' (DC1 or
CTRL/Q) code.  This allows the computer which is receiving data to stop the
transmission whenever it runs out of room in its input buffer.

     Aside from implementing this protocol, however, the user should note
that this handler also contains a number of other features tailored to the
'peculiarities' of several DEC operating systems:

     1) Linefeeds and nulls are suppressed during output (see preceeding dis-
cussion of the '/L' option in the HOSTX program).

     2) CTRL/Z (the OS/8 end-of-file character) is normally sent to the host
in order to close the file.  This limits COPY operations to one-file-at-a-
time.  The user may, however, set the 'no-EOF' switch so that several OS/8
files can be merged into one host file.

     3) The handler uses a time-out loop to detect the end-of-transmission
from the host.  The response time of this loop can be adjusted to accomodate
different processors and/or different system activity levels.

     4) The handler sends 'XON+RETURN' when it is ready to receive data from
the host.  It then waits for a 'LINEFEED' plus a 'RETURN' before storing data
in the buffer.  These characters are not part of the data file, but are gen-
erated in response to the initial 'return'.

     5) Since the host does not stop sending characters immediately after re-
ceiving XOFF, the handler has been designed to store up to 8 'overflow' char-
acters in an internal buffer.  These characters are transferred to the user's
input buffer the next time the handler is called.  This feature limits usage
of the handler with OS/8 FORTRAN IV programs to situations in which no other
OS/8 handlers are active at the same time, with the exception of operations
involving only the system device.

     6) Multiple 'linefeeds' during input are converted to multiple 'CR,LF'
pairs.  This solves a problem with early versions of VMS in which consecutive
blank lines (= multiple CR's in the OS/8 world) were sent as a single CR fol-
lowed by multiple LF's.  Since most OS/8 programs discard LF, the result was
the loss of all blank lines.

     7) Current versions of VMS seem to send multiple CR's.  For example, the
response to a DCL command is 'CR,LF,CR', with no apparent way to avoid the
second CR.  Be sure to set both the /CRFILL and /LFFILL parameters to '0' to
avoid other spurious 'returns'.  You should also set the /FORM option so that
formfeeds will be preserved.

